<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anita Garibaldi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ana Maria de Jesus Ribeiro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anita <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>, born Ana Maria de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> Ribeiro in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-catarina">Santa Catarina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, was the companion and later wife of Giuseppe Garibaldi. She met him at Laguna in 1839 and fought beside him in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragamuffin-war">Ragamuffin War</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> and in the defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in 1849. She died during the retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anita-garibaldi.jpg" alt="Anita Garibaldi" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gaetano Gallino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anita_Garibaldi_-_1839.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
